A||||13||72vinman||How can I prolong the life of my weatherstripping???||||5||
Z||000000||72vinman||08-30-2001||11:51 PM||||Does anyone know what i can put on the weatherstripping to soften it up and make it last a little longer? Around the doors it is in real good shape but in some spots it getting a little dry.<P>------------------<BR>72vinman@home.com<BR>Stock Black 86GT<BR> <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/gtaclub/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.members.home.net/gtaclub/</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||08-31-2001||02:12 AM||||I use mink oil on mine. It's the same stuff for leather coats and Leather seats. It keeps the seals soft and flexible without being greasy or attracting dirt. It also repels water so your doors won't freeze shut in the winter. It even helps prevent sunroof leaks too. I do all the seals on all my cars every fall.<BR>Just clean the seals with a good cleaner. Even soap and water works to clean them. Dry them and rub in the mink oil. (It comes in a paste form)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||08-31-2001||02:18 AM||||Mink oil...Where do you get it?  What is is really used for?<P>I've just been using Armor All.<BR>Joe<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||08-31-2001||02:55 AM||||Mink oil is for leather products like shoes or coats.  You can purchase it at any shoe repair store, or any place that sells leather stuff.  Every mall has one of these!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Primaris||08-31-2001||02:55 AM||||I use Armor All.  Mink oil is sold just about anywhere you can buy shoe polish.  It comes in a can similiar to black kiwi.  Try Wal Mart.  It's normaly use on dark leather products to keep them soft and to add some water repellent protection.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIERO JOHN-WI||08-31-2001||07:53 AM||||Avon skin so soft is what alot of Wisconsin Fiero Fanatics use my self included I put it on the side walls of the tires,weatherstripping, door seals and even on the Fiero that I don't drive. Even my 88 t-top with 18,604 miles with orginal tires and I purchased this car in Feb. 1990 and still no crack in the tires. Note I put alot on and use my hands to rub it in (I never use a rag or towel) I have used the spray and the lotion so either will work(but I prefer the lotion.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-31-2001||08:33 PM||||The areas that are already damaged will stay damaged no matter what you do to them...<P>You can use various things to protect them from more damage.<P>Mink oil certainly would not hurt them. I've used that before on leather. It might could help on seals. It would at least keep dirt and water from reacting on the material. Problem I see there is that the rubber doesn't absorb it like leather. Seems like it would wash off fairly easy. I'm not sure if I still have any around... I'll have to try it some time and see what happens.<P>I usually try to find something that will repell crap and provide UV protection.<P>To clean you want a mild soap. I usually clean with Simple Green. After that I've been using Son of a Gun. That doesn't leave that greasy feeling like Armor All.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||08-31-2001||10:06 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B><P>I've just been using Armor All.<BR>Joe<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>EEEEEEEEWWWWWWWWWW I keep telling you guys to throw that stuff away.......again. Your lucky that it hasnt already started cracking and splitting. New car dealers and me too, just use silicone spray on a cloth and wipe it on. <P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||formula2||08-31-2001||10:17 PM||||I spray them with silicone. You can buy a big can at cdn tire for a few bucks. <BR>Good for my winter car too.. ||||reg||6
Z||000009||fierospeeder||09-01-2001||12:15 AM||||ive read either silicone spray, or petroleum jelly, aka vaseline.||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||09-02-2001||12:24 AM||||I use Formula 2001 (I belive Turtle wax makes this, comes in a VERY GREEN spray bottle  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> on just about every surface, interior (leaves very little/no residue, so you can use it on surfaces you touch... sparingly.) all rubber seals/striping, and the engine itself, as well as the decklid and hood and front compartment/shock towers, etc.<P>Been using it for years, absolutely no problems, and it's probally all that has prevented my formula's interior from cracking/buldging/getting-ugly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It's got all kinds of good stuff, basically everything that is leeched out of plastics, rubbers, and some paints over time, as well as UV and heat protectant.  <P>(not appropriate for leather use, I belive.  I use different things for my leather  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000011||Joe Torma||09-02-2001||02:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR>[B]  EEEEEEEEWWWWWWWWWW I keep telling you guys to throw that stuff away.......again. Your lucky that it hasnt already started cracking and splitting. New car dealers and me too, just use silicone spray on a cloth and wipe it on.B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Now I know better...didn't know what else to use.<P>Sort of on the subject....does/is anybody make the door weatherstripping?  Mine are ok--no cracks, but the bottom is getting a little distorted.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||ka4nkf||09-03-2001||10:53 PM||||try The FIERo store for the weatherstripping||||reg||1
